\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Codecs in flight and planned} --- configurations, not results. Parameters are measured from the model rather than taken from the recipe's name, which is why the ``40M'' and ``70M'' families read 38.0M and 71.3M here. All eleven are on the pentad r2 tensor (\texttt  {family4\_na025\_pentad\_r2}, $C{=}40$, $T{=}3142$) with the longitude holdout retired (\S  \ref {sec:nolonhold}) and \texttt  {patch} 1; the month-block rows are $512\times 12$ / $d_{\mathrm  {dec}}$ 256 at 38.0M and $1024\times 10$ with 8 heads / $d_{\mathrm  {dec}}$ 768 at 126.9M, the E-046/E-049/E-050 rows share the $512\times 12$ / $d_{\mathrm  {dec}}$ 256 geometry at their stated $d_z$, and the E-048 rows are $768\times 10$ with 8 heads / $d_{\mathrm  {dec}}$ 512. \emph  {One $z$ per} is the unit the codec emits and therefore the step the forecaster takes, which is the whole argument of \S  \ref {sec:cadence}: the 5-day-bin rows keep the pentad step, the month-block and 30-day-window rows re-coarsen it to monthly, and only the final row emits faster than monthly while carrying a month of context. \emph  {Tier} matters because a JAX-trained number and a PyTorch-trained one are not pooled (\S  \ref {sec:jax}). No number from any of these rows may be written as a level --- each is a new codec, and several are a new codec, a new axis and a new bottleneck at once, which puts every one of them in the class where the first result buys its own replication.}}{51}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:inflight}{{9}{51}{\textbf {Codecs in flight and planned} --- configurations, not results. Parameters are measured from the model rather than taken from the recipe's name, which is why the ``40M'' and ``70M'' families read 38.0M and 71.3M here. All eleven are on the pentad r2 tensor (\texttt {family4\_na025\_pentad\_r2}, $C{=}40$, $T{=}3142$) with the longitude holdout retired (\S \ref {sec:nolonhold}) and \texttt {patch} 1; the month-block rows are $512\times 12$ / $d_{\mathrm {dec}}$ 256 at 38.0M and $1024\times 10$ with 8 heads / $d_{\mathrm {dec}}$ 768 at 126.9M, the E-046/E-049/E-050 rows share the $512\times 12$ / $d_{\mathrm {dec}}$ 256 geometry at their stated $d_z$, and the E-048 rows are $768\times 10$ with 8 heads / $d_{\mathrm {dec}}$ 512. \emph {One $z$ per} is the unit the codec emits and therefore the step the forecaster takes, which is the whole argument of \S \ref {sec:cadence}: the 5-day-bin rows keep the pentad step, the month-block and 30-day-window rows re-coarsen it to monthly, and only the final row emits faster than monthly while carrying a month of context. \emph {Tier} matters because a JAX-trained number and a PyTorch-trained one are not pooled (\S \ref {sec:jax}). No number from any of these rows may be written as a level --- each is a new codec, and several are a new codec, a new axis and a new bottleneck at once, which puts every one of them in the class where the first result buys its own replication}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Every one-variable arm at pentad cadence}, one row each. ``vs'' names the measured cell the arm differs from by exactly the stated variable. Ratios are one-step $z$-space MSE over each arm's own persistence baseline at 20k steps; lower is better. The factorial block's reading is \S  \ref {sec:factorial}'s: contrasts that move only the step change the ratio by ${\sim }4\%$, contrasts that move only the span change it by $4$--$7\times $. Run keys, curves and dispatch configurations for every row are in the public experiment log under E-044/E-045.}}{52}{table.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablations}{{10}{52}{\textbf {Every one-variable arm at pentad cadence}, one row each. ``vs'' names the measured cell the arm differs from by exactly the stated variable. Ratios are one-step $z$-space MSE over each arm's own persistence baseline at 20k steps; lower is better. The factorial block's reading is \S \ref {sec:factorial}'s: contrasts that move only the step change the ratio by ${\sim }4\%$, contrasts that move only the span change it by $4$--$7\times $. Run keys, curves and dispatch configurations for every row are in the public experiment log under E-044/E-045}{table.10}{}}
